description: Found in review of #118. Native lg builds a record's entries as plain vectors (`Record.Seq` makes `ArrayVector`s), so `map-entry?` is false and `key`/`val` raise "val expects map entry". The wasm runtime builds them through `m/entries->map-seq` as map entries (kind 23), so `map-entry?` is true and `key`/`val` answer. Probe at main f1f6d8f plus #118:
 
     (defrecord R [a b]) (def r (->R 1 2))
     (prn (map-entry? (first r)) (vector? (first r)) (type (first r)))
     (prn (try (key (first r)) (catch Exception e :raised)) (try (val (first r)) (catch Exception e :raised)))
 
 native prints `false true let-go.lang.ArrayVector` then `:raised :raised`; wasm prints `true true let-go.lang.ArrayVector` then `:a 1`. The corpus does not see it: `records.lg` reads entries through `first`/`second` and `type`, which agree.
 
 Which side moves is open: Clojure gives a record map entries, so native may be the one to change upstream; until then the oracle relation says wasm follows native.
 
 Facts that close it
 F1 the probe above prints the same two lines natively and under `checks/wasm-run.sh`.
 F2 `corpus/wasm/records.lg` has a line with `map-entry?`, `key` and `val` on a record entry, and `checks/oracle.sh corpus/wasm/records.lg` exits 0.
 
 Related from the same review: `sortable?`, `coll?`, `seqable-k?` and `callable?` in rt/wasm/core.lg each keep their own ckind list, which is how #76 missed `sort`; one shared collection-kind predicate would stop the next miss.
